# Version-Disabled Screens

These workspaces are shipped in code but must stay unreachable for every user and every
subscription package until a later version re-enables them.

## Disabled destinations

Emergency, Rooms & beds, Physiotherapy, Operations, Housekeeping, Biomedical Engineering,
Mortuary, Communications, Integrations.

That is 9 of the 32 route atoms in `route_access_catalog.dart`, leaving 23 live
destinations — 3 authenticated-core (`home`, `settings`, `profile`) and 20 of the 29
permission-gated atoms.

## Permission domains (hard deny)

`emergency`, `rooms_beds`, `physiotherapy`, `operations`, `housekeeping`, `biomed`,
`mortuary`, `communications`, `integration`.

Domain names, not module slugs — `biomed` (not `biomedical`) and `integration` (not
`integrations`). 25 of the 85 catalog keys fall in these domains.

## Enforcement

- Strip these domains from effective access on API and UI (including elevated roles and
  platform-owner paths that skip plan caps). Stripping is the **last** step of
  effective-access, after grants, modules and caps.
- Omit them from all `PLAN_PERMISSION_CAPS` / `PlanPermissionCaps` tiers.
- Menu items and routes stay permission-gated; denied keys must hide shell destinations.
- Do not re-grant via role packs, custom plans, module entitlements, or direct user grants.
  Role packs and the permission catalog may still **list** these keys — that is expected;
  the strip is what enforces the rule.
- To re-enable: remove the domain from `version-disabled-permissions` (BE + FE), add its
  keys to the appropriate `PLAN_PERMISSION_CAPS` / `PlanPermissionCaps` tier (at or above
  the module's minimum tier), then drop it from this rule. `DOMAIN_TO_MODULE` already maps
  every disabled domain — nothing was removed from it, so nothing needs restoring there.

## Modules stay entitled

The matching modules still ship and may be entitled by a package —
`notifications-communications` (BASIC), `physiotherapy` (ADVANCED),
`facilities-maintenance`, `biomedical-engineering-suite`, `mortuary`, `integrations-core`
(PRO). They simply grant nothing while their domain is stripped. Do not remove them from
`plan-module-matrix.js`; that would change package composition rather than defer a screen.

`scheduling-queue` and `inpatient-bed-management` are only *partly* affected — they carry
live domains (`reception`/`opd`, `ipd`/`nursing`/`discharge`) alongside disabled ones
(`emergency`, `rooms_beds`).

## Testing caveat

`frontend/test/flutter_test_config.dart` sets `VersionDisabledPermissions.enforce = false`
for the whole `test/` suite so deferred-screen feature tests can still exercise their
permission matrices. Deferred-domain denial is therefore **not** asserted by default. A
test that needs to prove a disabled key is denied must set `enforce = true` itself and
restore it — see `frontend/test/core/permissions/version_disabled_permissions_test.dart`.

